hen (the former triangle) will also have a base greater than the base (of the latter).\"\n\nSo, according to the theorem:\n- The equal sides are AB = DE and AC = DF (not AB = DE and AC = DF).\n- The angle at A (∠BAC) is greater than the angle at D (∠EDF).\n\nThus, in the formal statement:\n- The side AB corresponds to |(a─b)|.\n- The side AC corresponds to |(a─c)|.\n- The angle at A corresponds to (∠ b:a:c : ℝ).\n\nBut in the conclusion: the base BC corresponds to |(b─c)| and EF to |(e─f)|.\n\nWait, in the theorem, the angle at A is angle BAC, and the angle at D is angle EDF. The sides AB and AC are equal to DE and DF. The theorem is saying that if angle at A is greater than angle at D, then side BC is greater than side EF.\n\nSo I should adjust the theorem following the given constraints.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ)) : |(b─c)| > |(e─f)| := by sorry\n```\n\nHmm, but according to the theorem statement, the angle at A is ∠BAC, which is formed by points b, a, c. The angle at D is ∠EDF, formed by points e, d, f.\n\nBut according to the theorem, angle at A (∠BAC) is greater than angle at D (∠EDF). So the angle ∠b:a:c (BAC) is greater than angle ∠e:d:f (EDF).\n\nThus, the theorem is correctly formalized as:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ)) : |(b─c)| > |(e─f)| := by sorry\n```\n\nBut, just to be sure, I should double-check:\n- The theorem states two triangles with sides AB = DE and AC = DF.\n- Angle at A (∠BAC) > angle at D (∠EDF).\n- Conclusion: side BC > side EF.\n\nSo in the formalization:\n- h3 is |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|, representing AB = DE.\n- h4 is |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|, representing AC = DF.\n- h5 is (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ), representing ∠BAC > ∠EDF.\n- The conclusion is |(b─c)| > |(e─f)|, representing BC > EF.\n\nThis seems correct. The theorem is about the side opposite the angle being larger if the angle is larger, given the other two sides are equal. This is consistent with the properties of triangles.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ)) : |(b─c)| > |(e─f)| := by sorry\n```": 0.10998427875786934, "Alright, I need to formalize this theorem from Euclidean geometry. Let me first try to understand the English statement clearly.\n\nThe theorem states: If two triangles have two sides equal to two sides, respectively, but one has the angle encompassed by the equal straight lines greater than the corresponding angle in the other, then the base of the former triangle is greater than the base of the latter.\n\nSo, we have two triangles, ABC and DEF. In triangle ABC, sides AB and AC are equal to sides DE and DF, respectively, in triangle DEF. But the angle at A (the angle between AB and AC) is greater than the angle at D (the angle between DE and DF). The theorem claims that then the base BC is greater than the base EF.\n\nOkay, that makes sense. Now, I need to formalize this in Lean 4. Let me look at the structure of the theorem and the provided examples.\n\nFirst, the theorem should state what the given conditions are and what we need to prove.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm\n```\n\nNow, I need to define the variables. We have two triangles: ABC and DEF. So, points a, b, c, d, e, f. Also, the lines AB, AC, BC, DE, DF, EF.\n\n```tactics\n(a b c d e f : Point)\n(AB AC BC DE DF EF : Line)\n```\n\nNow, the hypotheses.\n\nFirst, that ABC is a triangle. So, points a, b, c form a triangle, with the sides AB, BC, and AC.\n\n```tactics\n(h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n```\n\nSimilarly, that DEF is a triangle.\n\n```tactics\n(h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF)\n```\n\nNow, that |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|. The length of side AB equals the length of side DE.\n\n```tactics\n(h3 : (|(a─b)| : ℝ) = (|(d─e)| : ℝ))\n```\n\nSimilarly, that |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|. The length of side AC equals the length of side DF.\n\n```tactics\n(h4 : (|(a─c)| : ℝ) = (|(d─f)| : ℝ))\n```\n\nThen, that angle at A > angle at D. So, ∠ a:b:c > ∠ d:e:f.\n\nWait, the angle at A is ∠a:b:c? Wait, in the standard notation, triangle ABC has points A, B, C. So, the angle at A is ∠BAC, which would be ∠ b:a:c in the Lean notation. Similarly, the angle at D would be ∠ e:d:f.\n\nWait, that's confusing. Let me think. If we have points a, b, c, then the angle at a is formed by points b, a, c. So, angle ∠ b:a:c. Similarly, in triangle DEF, the angle at d is formed by points e, d, f. So, angle ∠ e:d:f.\n\nSo, the hypothesis that angle at A > angle at D becomes:\n\n```tactics\n(h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ))\n```\n\nNow, the conclusion: that base BC > base EF. So, |(b─c)| > |(e─f)|.\n\n```tactics\n: (|(b─c)| : ℝ) > (|(e─f)| : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\nSo, putting all together, the theorem statement would be:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm\n(a b c d e f : Point)\n(AB AC BC DE DF EF : Line)\n(h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n(h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF)\n(h3 : (|(a─b)| : ℝ) = (|(d─e)| : ℝ))\n(h4 : (|(a─c)| : ℝ) = (|(d─f)| : ℝ))\n(h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ))\n: (|(b─c)| : ℝ) > (|(e─f)| : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\nHmm, I need to check if this is consistent with the provided examples. Looking at the examples, they have things like (∠ d₁:d₂:d₃ : ℝ). So, angle at d₂ between points d₁, d₂, d₃. Similarly here, angle ∠ b:a:c is the angle at a between points b, a, c. That's standard.\n\nAlso, they use distinctPointsOnLine, but I'm not using that here. But in a formTriangle, the points are distinct, implicitly. So, triangle implies that the points are distinct.\n\nSo, this seems reasonable. The theorem takes two triangles with sides equal but angles different and needs to prove that the base is larger when the angle is larger.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm\n(a b c d e f : Point)\n(AB AC BC DE DF EF : Line)\n(h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n(h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF)\n(h3 : (|(a─b)| : ℝ) = (|(d─e)| : ℝ))\n(h4 : (|(a─c)| : ℝ) = (|(d─f)| : ℝ))\n(h5 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > (∠ e:d:f : ℝ))\n: (|(b─c)| : ℝ) > (|(e─f)| : ℝ) := by sorry\n```": 0.10990458877381216}, "cleaned_response": "∀ (a : Point) (b : Point) (c : Point) (d : Point) (e : Point) (f : Point) (AB : Line) (BC : Line) (AC : Line) (DE : Line) (EF : Line) (DF : Line), ((formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) ∧ (formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) ∧ (|(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) ∧ (|(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) ∧ ((∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > ∠ e:d:f)) → ((|(b─c)| > |(e─f)|))", "prediction": {"∀ (a : Point) (b : Point) (c : Point) (d : Point) (e : Point) (f : Point) (AB : Line) (BC : Line) (AC : Line) (DE : Line) (EF : Line) (DF : Line), ((formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) ∧ (formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) ∧ (|(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) ∧ (|(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) ∧ ((∠ b:a:c : ℝ) > ∠ e:d:f)) → ((|(b─c)| > |(e─f)|))": 1.0}, "groud_truth": "∀ (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line), formTriangle a b c AB BC AC ∧ formTriangle d e f DE EF DF ∧ (|(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) ∧ (|(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) ∧ (∠ b:a:c > ∠ e:d:f) → |(b─c)| > |(e─f)|"}